1
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:12,860
In this module, we will continue our discussion regarding approach to interpretation of chest radiographs

2
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:17,920
with discussion of evaluation of the diaphragm, pleura, and chest wall.

3
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:24,620
Following completion of this module, the learner should be able to employ a systematic search pattern

4
00:00:24,620 --> 00:00:27,040
for interpreting chest radiographs,

5
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:32,960
identify commonly encountered abnormalities of the diaphragm, pleura, and chest wall,

6
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:40,460
and describe the relative sensitivities of various radiographic views in the detection of pleural effusions.

7
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:48,460
Returning to our inside-out approach, in the first module, we discussed assessment of line and tube placement,

8
00:00:49,220 --> 00:00:54,020
evaluation of the trachea and large central airways, as well as the mediastinum and heart.

9
00:00:54,900 --> 00:01:01,120
We're going to continue this search pattern in this module with discussion of the diaphragm and pleura.

10
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:07,420
Because assessment of the lungs takes quite a bit more time, we're going to skip the lungs,

11
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:09,760
and that'll be a separate module in module 3.

12
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:13,860
And then we're going to skip on to the bones and soft tissue.

13
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:18,860
So in this module, we'll discuss the diaphragm, pleura, and bones and soft tissues.

14
00:01:20,380 --> 00:01:23,820
Now remember in our search pattern, we started on the inside.

15
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:28,500
Lines and tubes, trachea and airways, mediastinum, heart, and hyla.

16
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:32,980
Now we're going to start to move towards the outside.

17
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:38,140
So we're going to have our eyes follow the diaphragm from medial to lateral.

18
00:01:39,180 --> 00:01:43,020
One of the major things we're going to be looking for is what's the height of the diaphragm.

19
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:45,920
And typically the right is higher than the left is shown here.

20
00:01:46,620 --> 00:01:52,140
In addition, we want to assess the location of the apex of the diaphragm.

21
00:01:52,140 --> 00:01:56,720
So the apex or the highest point that it appears on the frontal view

22
00:01:56,720 --> 00:02:00,820
is typically a little bit closer to the midline than it is lateral.

23
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:07,700
So in this example, we have the apex of the right and the apex of the left hemidiaphragms here.

24
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:11,600
So these are usually about a third of the way from the midline to the lateral chest wall.

25
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:16,660
In addition, as we go from pure lung to pure abdominal soft tissues,

26
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:24,480
we should see this gradation of opacity deep to the apex of the diaphragm.

27
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:30,780
And what we're seeing here is more and more soft tissue and less and less lung

28
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:34,480
as we go from the thoracic cavity to the abdominal cavity.

29
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:40,960
If it's a well-penetrated examination, you are sometimes able to identify lung vessels

30
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:46,920
actually through the diaphragm from aerated lung outlining these vessels,

31
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:54,780
both anterior and posterior, to the apex of the diaphragm in the anterior and posterior sulci.

32
00:02:55,320 --> 00:03:00,460
And then finally, while we're here, we should look deep to the diaphragms

33
00:03:00,460 --> 00:03:03,900
to assess for any free intraperitoneal gas that you might see,

34
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:07,040
especially on an upright chest radiograph.

35
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:13,900
Now let's look at some potential causes of an elevation or at least apparent elevation of the diaphragm,

36
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:17,020
beginning with discussion of a subpalmonic effusion.

37
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:22,240
Now the term subpalmonic effusion refers to fluid collecting at the base of the lung,

38
00:03:22,760 --> 00:03:28,960
between the lung base and the diaphragm in an upright or semi-upright patient.

39
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:35,500
So rather than blunting the costophrenic angle, which is what typically happens,

40
00:03:35,980 --> 00:03:41,300
in this case, you get what looks like an apparent elevation of the diaphragm,

41
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:45,720
secondary to fluid collecting between the diaphragm and the lung base.

42
00:03:45,720 --> 00:03:52,220
So what are some of the radiographic clues that this apparent elevation of the diaphragm is really a subpalmonic effusion?

43
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:58,560
Well, the first is lateralization of the apparent apex of the diaphragm.

44
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:05,660
So in cases of subpalmonic effusion, this gets moved more laterally.

45
00:04:05,660 --> 00:04:10,260
So we talked about it on the previous slide is that apex usually being about a third of the way

46
00:04:10,260 --> 00:04:14,060
between the midline and lateral chest wall, so a little bit more medial.

47
00:04:14,340 --> 00:04:18,700
Now it gets pushed laterally, so the apparent apex looks more lateral.

48
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:24,120
In addition, instead of that gradation of opacity behind the diaphragm that we talked about,

49
00:04:24,660 --> 00:04:32,720
you actually have a uniform opacity because now you're just visualizing the fluid beneath the lung base,

50
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:34,880
as shown there.

51
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:39,960
You will also lose visualization of the lung vessels.

52
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:45,200
Now if there's any question about, okay, is this really a subpalmonic effusion or not,

53
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:49,100
you can do a lateral decubitus image, and we'll talk about this a little bit later.

54
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:54,080
You want to put the affected side down, so in this case, you would ask for a right side down

55
00:04:54,080 --> 00:05:01,960
or left side up decubitus view to see if this fluid layers along the dependent thoracic cavity

56
00:05:01,960 --> 00:05:05,000
to confirm that there is, in fact, pleural fluid present.

57
00:05:07,190 --> 00:05:09,990
Eventration of the diaphragm can cause elevation.

58
00:05:10,950 --> 00:05:17,430
This refers to a focal contour deformity in the hemidiaphragm due to a congenital absence

59
00:05:17,430 --> 00:05:19,510
of the diaphragmatic muscle.

60
00:05:19,670 --> 00:05:24,530
So usually that muscle is replaced by this membranous sheath, which is weak

61
00:05:24,530 --> 00:05:27,270
and allows a focal contour deformity.

62
00:05:27,270 --> 00:05:33,270
In this case, notice the apparent double contour of the right hemidiaphragm.

63
00:05:33,610 --> 00:05:40,870
This is that same case of eventration showing that double contour of the right diaphragm.

64
00:05:42,230 --> 00:05:49,330
A sagittal reconstructed view of the right diaphragm in this patient shows that, in fact,

65
00:05:49,370 --> 00:05:54,730
there is this focal eventration or weakening of the diaphragm that allows the liver to kind

66
00:05:54,730 --> 00:05:58,170
of pooch up into the thoracic cavity.

67
00:05:59,350 --> 00:06:04,770
The normal diaphragm, in this case, is posterior so that you can see on a frontal radiograph

68
00:06:04,770 --> 00:06:08,270
why you have this kind of double contour.

69
00:06:10,550 --> 00:06:15,950
Weakness or paralysis of the diaphragm can lead to elevation of the diaphragm.

70
00:06:15,950 --> 00:06:23,930
Unlike eventration, which also leads to elevation of the diaphragm, diaphragmatic paralysis typically

71
00:06:23,930 --> 00:06:29,790
will affect the entire hemidiaphragm and not be a focal abnormality like most eventrations.

72
00:06:30,410 --> 00:06:37,930
In this example, we have a frontal and lateral chest radiograph showing elevation of the right diaphragm,

73
00:06:38,310 --> 00:06:42,150
in this case secondary to diaphragmatic paralysis.

74
00:06:42,150 --> 00:06:57,320
In this case, test your knowledge of etiologies of elevation or at least apparent elevation of the diaphragm.

75
00:06:57,980 --> 00:07:02,440
In this example, the right hemidiaphragm is elevated.

76
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:07,740
Notice that there's really no focal contour abnormality to suggest an eventration.

77
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:11,760
In addition, there are no findings of subpalmonic effusion.

78
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:18,700
So if we look as we go from the lung to the abdomen, we have that normal gradation of opacity

79
00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:24,800
from relatively lucent to relatively opaque as we go from the lung to the abdomen.

80
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:32,880
Also notice that deep to the apex of the diaphragm, we can identify lung vessels

81
00:07:32,880 --> 00:07:37,640
so that we know that these lung vessels have aerated lung around them.

82
00:07:37,640 --> 00:07:44,800
Unlike with subpalmonic effusion where you lose that gradation opacity and you obscure any vessels

83
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:53,420
that you might see deep to the diaphragm because of fluid collecting between the base of the lung

84
00:07:53,420 --> 00:07:57,340
and the diaphragm that you would see with subpalmonic effusion.

85
00:07:57,920 --> 00:08:02,640
So therefore, imaging findings in this case are most suggestive of diaphragmatic paralysis.

86
00:08:02,640 --> 00:08:07,700
As we move from medial to lateral, evaluating the diaphragms,

87
00:08:08,640 --> 00:08:13,800
our eyes will be at the level of the lateral costophenic sulci to assess for pleural effusions.

88
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:20,040
And then we'll move up along the lateral chest wall from base to apex,

89
00:08:20,740 --> 00:08:25,060
assessing the pleura for things like thickening, fluid, or pneumothoraces.

90
00:08:25,060 --> 00:08:30,720
So the pleural space is a potential space that can fill either with fluid,

91
00:08:31,360 --> 00:08:36,120
which we term a pleural effusion, or gas, which we term a pneumothorax,

92
00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:38,560
or a combination of fluid and gas.

93
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:43,660
Now the important thing here is that the appearance of both an effusion and a pneumothorax

94
00:08:43,660 --> 00:08:48,540
will depend on the patient positioning at the time of imaging.

95
00:08:48,540 --> 00:08:56,200
So we'll talk about the appearance of effusions and pneumothoraces on both supine and upright images.

96
00:08:56,500 --> 00:08:59,780
In addition, we'll talk about the use of decubitus views,

97
00:08:59,880 --> 00:09:03,780
particularly for assessment of pleural effusions.

98
00:09:06,030 --> 00:09:10,510
Pleural effusion refers to fluid within the pleural space.

99
00:09:11,170 --> 00:09:16,350
Now that fluid will collect in the dependent portion of the pleural cavity.

100
00:09:16,350 --> 00:09:21,330
The dependent portion of the cavity will vary depending on the position of the patient.

101
00:09:22,230 --> 00:09:28,770
So in an upright position, the most dependent portion of the pleural cavity will be at the lung base.

102
00:09:29,670 --> 00:09:37,670
So pleural effusions in the upright patient will manifest themselves as an increased density in the lung base.

103
00:09:38,510 --> 00:09:41,850
The fluid will spill out into the lateral costophenic sulcus,

104
00:09:41,850 --> 00:09:45,310
causing blunting of the lateral costophenic sulcus,

105
00:09:45,670 --> 00:09:47,930
as well as the meniscus effect,

106
00:09:48,270 --> 00:09:52,530
as the fluid layers along the thoracic wall.

107
00:09:53,330 --> 00:09:58,810
The example on the left shows a moderate to large left pleural effusion,

108
00:09:59,810 --> 00:10:02,370
with blunting of the lateral costophenic sulcus,

109
00:10:03,410 --> 00:10:06,430
and the meniscus effect as shown by the arrow.

110
00:10:06,430 --> 00:10:11,910
The image on the right shows small to moderate bilateral pleural effusions,

111
00:10:12,350 --> 00:10:17,310
with blunting of the costophenic angles bilaterally, as well as the meniscus effect.

112
00:10:20,750 --> 00:10:22,970
In an upright patient on a lateral view,

113
00:10:23,990 --> 00:10:28,510
pleural fluid will first be detected in the posterior costophenic angles.

114
00:10:29,090 --> 00:10:32,010
So you'll have blunting of the posterior costophenic sulci.

115
00:10:32,010 --> 00:10:39,550
Because the posterior costophenic sulci are the most dependent portion of the cavity,

116
00:10:40,030 --> 00:10:44,850
so even more dependent than the lateral costophenic angles identified on the frontal view,

117
00:10:45,990 --> 00:10:51,630
you're able to detect smaller amounts of pleural fluid on the lateral view as compared to the frontal view.

118
00:10:52,150 --> 00:10:56,470
In other words, fluid will collect in that posterior costophenic sulcus,

119
00:10:56,470 --> 00:11:02,590
and will be detected before there's enough fluid to spill out to cause blunting of the lateral costophenic angles,

120
00:11:02,710 --> 00:11:04,130
which we can detect on a frontal view.

121
00:11:04,270 --> 00:11:05,950
So let's look at an example of this.

122
00:11:06,410 --> 00:11:14,970
The image on the left is a frontal view with well-maintained lateral costophenic angles bilaterally.

123
00:11:15,810 --> 00:11:23,870
However, on the lateral view, there is blunting of the right posterior costophenic angle.

124
00:11:23,870 --> 00:11:29,850
So remember on the lateral view, the right ribs are magnified and project posterior to the left ribs

125
00:11:29,850 --> 00:11:33,210
because the left side is closer to the detector,

126
00:11:33,330 --> 00:11:37,210
so there's more magnification of the right side of the body.

127
00:11:37,830 --> 00:11:42,490
So here we can see the larger and more posteriorly projecting ribs are the right ribs,

128
00:11:42,750 --> 00:11:48,010
and if we look closely, we can see that there's no right posterior costophenic sulcus.

129
00:11:48,010 --> 00:11:59,370
In fact, it's blunted from the presence of pleural fluid, which we can see layering along the posterior margin of the pleural cavity

130
00:11:59,370 --> 00:12:01,070
because of that meniscus effect.

131
00:12:01,650 --> 00:12:07,210
So here we have a small amount of pleural fluid that's detected on the lateral view,

132
00:12:07,490 --> 00:12:09,750
collecting in that posterior costophenic sulcus,

133
00:12:09,750 --> 00:12:15,910
that is not yet visible on the frontal view because we don't have enough fluid to spill out

134
00:12:15,910 --> 00:12:21,370
into the lateral costophenic angles to cause blunting that can be detected on the frontal view.

135
00:12:23,810 --> 00:12:26,910
In the supine patient, pleural effusions look a little different

136
00:12:26,910 --> 00:12:34,690
because the effusion actually now layers along the posterior pleural cavity.

137
00:12:34,690 --> 00:12:38,490
So in the upright patient, all the fluid will collect at the lung base,

138
00:12:38,750 --> 00:12:44,190
but when that same patient then goes supine, that fluid will layer out.

139
00:12:44,350 --> 00:12:46,630
Now most of the fluid will stay at the lung base,

140
00:12:46,990 --> 00:12:54,970
and as we move from base to apex, as that fluid layers, it becomes less and less in volume,

141
00:12:55,130 --> 00:12:58,950
so the column becomes thinner and thinner from base to apex,

142
00:12:58,950 --> 00:13:05,710
so this results in sort of a graded opacity or opacity gradient from opaque to lucent

143
00:13:05,710 --> 00:13:07,970
as we go from base to apex.

144
00:13:08,390 --> 00:13:10,790
So that fluid will layer posteriorly.

145
00:13:10,950 --> 00:13:16,570
As we see in this example, you'll have this gradation of fluid more opaque at the base

146
00:13:16,570 --> 00:13:21,490
and becomes less and less opaque or more lucent as we go to the apex,

147
00:13:21,490 --> 00:13:29,070
and in addition, you have the diaphragms are obscured by the adjacent pleural fluid,

148
00:13:29,190 --> 00:13:31,670
so we don't see well-defined diaphragms.

149
00:13:31,730 --> 00:13:39,270
So this layering effect and graded opacity is characteristic for a pleural effusion in a supine patient.

150
00:13:40,810 --> 00:13:45,890
Now let's look at how decubitus imaging may be helpful in detection of pleural fluid.

151
00:13:45,890 --> 00:13:52,670
In this example, we have a frontal view of the chest in an upright position, essentially.

152
00:13:52,810 --> 00:13:55,550
The patient is sitting, and this is an AP radiograph.

153
00:13:56,470 --> 00:13:59,550
Notice that there's abnormal opacity at the left lung base,

154
00:13:59,830 --> 00:14:05,190
but really it's difficult to know if this is a pleural effusion or just some atelectasis or airspace disease

155
00:14:05,190 --> 00:14:06,830
or maybe a combination of the two.

156
00:14:07,410 --> 00:14:13,410
So if we want to determine if there is a fluid component to this opacity,

157
00:14:13,410 --> 00:14:19,430
that is, is there at least a component of pleural effusion, how might we do this?

158
00:14:20,310 --> 00:14:27,510
Well, we can use a decubitus view, and what we do here is we put the potentially affected side in the dependent position

159
00:14:27,510 --> 00:14:32,110
so that if fluid is present, it will layer along the thorax.

160
00:14:32,730 --> 00:14:35,850
So here we're thinking there may be a small left pleural effusion,

161
00:14:35,850 --> 00:14:40,990
so we want to do a left side down lateral decubitus view, as we see here.

162
00:14:42,190 --> 00:14:47,670
And notice now the opacity layering along the dependent portion of the thoracic cavity

163
00:14:47,670 --> 00:14:51,590
that represents layering pleural effusion fluid,

164
00:14:51,970 --> 00:14:56,790
so that we can be sure that at least part of that opacity on that frontal view

165
00:14:56,790 --> 00:14:59,690
is secondary to a pleural effusion.

166
00:14:59,690 --> 00:15:08,670
So decubitus views are the most sensitive way to detect pleural effusions on chest radiography.

167
00:15:09,990 --> 00:15:15,710
Regarding pleural effusion detection, CT and ultrasound are more sensitive than chest radiography.

168
00:15:16,770 --> 00:15:23,030
Decubitus chest radiographs are the most sensitive view in terms of chest radiography

169
00:15:23,030 --> 00:15:29,590
for detection of pleural effusions, followed by the upright lateral view, the upright frontal view,

170
00:15:29,590 --> 00:15:31,850
and then finally the supine view.

171
00:15:32,970 --> 00:15:37,630
You can confirm the presence as well as the mobility of pleural fluid

172
00:15:37,630 --> 00:15:42,230
with an ipsilateral decubitus view or with ultrasound.

173
00:15:42,990 --> 00:15:47,530
So let's say you have a patient that has a supine radiograph.

174
00:15:47,530 --> 00:15:53,230
There's a question of a left pleural effusion if you wanted to determine if that's real

175
00:15:53,230 --> 00:15:56,150
and if it is real, whether or not it's free-flowing or not.

176
00:15:56,590 --> 00:16:02,090
You can do a left side down decubitus view to see if it layers along the dependent portion of the thorax,

177
00:16:02,330 --> 00:16:04,230
or you can do an ultrasound.

178
00:16:06,030 --> 00:16:08,690
Now let's move on to detection of a pneumothorax.

179
00:16:09,470 --> 00:16:13,610
Pneumothorax refers to the collection of gas within the pleural space.

180
00:16:13,610 --> 00:16:20,170
Unlike fluid in the pleural space, which collects in the dependent portion of the space,

181
00:16:20,730 --> 00:16:23,670
gas will collect in the non-dependent portion.

182
00:16:24,150 --> 00:16:29,390
So just like a pleural effusion, the appearance of a pneumothorax on a radiograph

183
00:16:29,390 --> 00:16:32,450
will vary depending on the patient position.

184
00:16:33,390 --> 00:16:36,610
So the key here is to identify the position,

185
00:16:36,610 --> 00:16:44,490
and therefore then you would know the most non-dependent portion of the pleural space in that position.

186
00:16:44,630 --> 00:16:49,310
So in the upright position, the most non-dependent portion is in the lung apex,

187
00:16:49,570 --> 00:16:54,090
and this is where we would expect to identify a pneumothorax in an upright patient.

188
00:16:54,590 --> 00:17:00,010
The image on the left is a CT that shows a small right-sided pneumothorax.

189
00:17:00,010 --> 00:17:04,290
We can see that gas collects in the pleural space

190
00:17:04,290 --> 00:17:08,850
and displaces the visceral pleura away from the parietal pleura.

191
00:17:09,750 --> 00:17:17,450
And this displacement of the visceral pleura will be key to identifying a pneumothorax on a radiograph.

192
00:17:17,970 --> 00:17:21,130
The image on the right is a frontal radiograph,

193
00:17:21,130 --> 00:17:25,610
and it shows a curvilinear line at the left lung apex,

194
00:17:25,970 --> 00:17:32,130
which represents the displaced visceral pleural line.

195
00:17:35,620 --> 00:17:40,900
Now the appearance of the pneumothorax will vary depending on the status of the underlying lung.

196
00:17:41,320 --> 00:17:45,820
So if the underlying lung is completely collapsed,

197
00:17:45,820 --> 00:17:49,380
then a pneumothorax will appear as an interface

198
00:17:49,380 --> 00:17:55,340
between the abnormal lucency of the gas in the pleural space

199
00:17:55,340 --> 00:18:00,600
and the underlying completely collapsed and opaque lung.

200
00:18:01,200 --> 00:18:04,080
So for example, the image on the left,

201
00:18:05,380 --> 00:18:10,980
we have an interface between the pleural space and collapsed lung.

202
00:18:12,700 --> 00:18:16,480
However, if the lung remains aerated deep to the pneumothorax,

203
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:19,180
the pneumothorax will not appear as an interface,

204
00:18:19,780 --> 00:18:22,340
but will instead appear as a line.

205
00:18:22,540 --> 00:18:25,540
That's the inward displacement of the visceral pleural line

206
00:18:25,540 --> 00:18:29,020
that's detected as a line as shown here

207
00:18:29,020 --> 00:18:32,880
because there's gas in the pleural space on one side

208
00:18:32,880 --> 00:18:36,300
as well as gas within the lung parenchyma on the other.

209
00:18:38,750 --> 00:18:39,930
Now in a supine patient,

210
00:18:40,050 --> 00:18:42,230
the most non-dependent portion of the pleural cavity

211
00:18:42,230 --> 00:18:43,790
is actually at the lung base.

212
00:18:44,310 --> 00:18:48,030
So we would expect to detect pneumothoraces in supine patients

213
00:18:48,030 --> 00:18:52,650
at the lung base in contrast to the upright patient

214
00:18:52,650 --> 00:18:58,030
where we typically identify pneumothoraces in the lung apex.

215
00:18:58,510 --> 00:19:01,390
So in a supine patient to detect the pneumothorax,

216
00:19:01,810 --> 00:19:04,590
we're going to look for gas collecting at the lung base.

217
00:19:04,690 --> 00:19:06,830
So the lung base will look too lucent.

218
00:19:07,510 --> 00:19:09,350
Now sometimes this will manifest itself

219
00:19:09,350 --> 00:19:14,990
as a very sharply outlined cardiac or diaphragmatic contour.

220
00:19:14,990 --> 00:19:17,370
In other words, you're able to see the heart border

221
00:19:17,370 --> 00:19:20,790
or the border of the diaphragm much better than you typically would

222
00:19:20,790 --> 00:19:24,350
because the lung parenchyma is now replaced by pure gas

223
00:19:24,350 --> 00:19:26,050
in the pleural space.

224
00:19:26,550 --> 00:19:29,810
In addition, sometimes that gas at the lung base

225
00:19:29,810 --> 00:19:33,750
within the pleural space can actually dissect down

226
00:19:33,750 --> 00:19:37,330
into the lateral costophrinic sulcus

227
00:19:37,330 --> 00:19:39,710
and cause a lucent and deep sulcus.

228
00:19:39,790 --> 00:19:42,230
And this is termed the deep sulcus sign.

229
00:19:42,430 --> 00:19:46,510
It's indicative of a pneumothorax in a supine patient.

230
00:19:47,070 --> 00:19:48,950
So the example shown here,

231
00:19:49,350 --> 00:19:53,150
you can see that that right lateral costophrinic sulcus

232
00:19:53,150 --> 00:19:55,270
is deep and lucent as shown here.

233
00:19:55,870 --> 00:19:57,890
And this is an example of a patient

234
00:19:57,890 --> 00:20:02,430
with a right-sided pneumothorax in a supine position.

235
00:20:02,430 --> 00:20:05,250
And you can see adjacent ancillary findings

236
00:20:05,250 --> 00:20:07,430
of rib fractures and chest wall gas.

237
00:20:09,590 --> 00:20:12,050
Now anytime you detect a pneumothorax,

238
00:20:12,110 --> 00:20:14,070
you don't want to stop at near detection.

239
00:20:14,530 --> 00:20:15,990
You always have to ask yourself,

240
00:20:16,390 --> 00:20:20,350
do I see any signs of a tension pneumothorax in this patient?

241
00:20:20,410 --> 00:20:23,470
Because this would need to be treated emergently.

242
00:20:24,410 --> 00:20:28,130
So tension pneumothoraces are associated with mass effect

243
00:20:28,130 --> 00:20:32,630
from the abnormal collection of gas in the pleural space.

244
00:20:32,630 --> 00:20:36,090
So you're looking for signs of mass effect.

245
00:20:36,190 --> 00:20:40,430
So these include contralateral shift of the mediastinum.

246
00:20:40,710 --> 00:20:42,270
So for example, in this case,

247
00:20:42,990 --> 00:20:44,970
we have a right-sided pneumothorax.

248
00:20:45,010 --> 00:20:46,590
We can see the visceral pleural line

249
00:20:46,590 --> 00:20:51,510
and the lucent right thorax related to the pneumothorax itself.

250
00:20:52,890 --> 00:20:55,590
We have contralateral shift.

251
00:20:55,670 --> 00:20:58,530
So there's leftward shift from a right pneumothorax

252
00:20:58,530 --> 00:20:59,890
of the mediastinum.

253
00:20:59,890 --> 00:21:02,850
So we have contralateral mediastinal shift.

254
00:21:03,750 --> 00:21:08,210
The ipsilateral or the same side as which the pneumothorax is on.

255
00:21:08,310 --> 00:21:12,090
So the ipsilateral diaphragm is typically depressed as we see here.

256
00:21:12,350 --> 00:21:14,210
It's pushed down from the mass effect.

257
00:21:14,210 --> 00:21:22,590
And then sometimes you'll see actually an increased interspace rib distance on the ipsilateral side

258
00:21:22,590 --> 00:21:25,350
because basically the thorax is being expanded.

259
00:21:25,630 --> 00:21:29,990
So the rib distance will increase relative to the contralateral side.

260
00:21:30,670 --> 00:21:34,310
So this patient has a right tension pneumothorax.

261
00:21:34,310 --> 00:21:38,330
Also note that the lung base is too loosened.

262
00:21:38,750 --> 00:21:42,450
You see a very well-defined right diaphragm.

263
00:21:43,590 --> 00:21:45,590
And then you can see a deep sulcus.

264
00:21:46,870 --> 00:21:51,190
So this person has kind of all the findings of the supine pneumothorax

265
00:21:51,190 --> 00:21:55,870
as well as a tension component as evidenced by the associated mass effect.

266
00:21:55,870 --> 00:22:04,460
A pneumothorax refers to the presence of both fluid and gas within the pleural space.

267
00:22:05,100 --> 00:22:10,740
In this example, we have a frontal view on the left and a lateral view on the right,

268
00:22:12,660 --> 00:22:15,960
demonstrating a right-sided pneumothorax.

269
00:22:15,960 --> 00:22:23,540
We can identify the visceral pleural line that's displaced because of the presence of gas within the pleural space.

270
00:22:24,620 --> 00:22:30,880
And in addition, we have blunting of the right lateral as well as the poster costophrenic angles,

271
00:22:31,320 --> 00:22:35,880
secondary to fluid layering in the dependent portion of the thoracic cavity,

272
00:22:36,300 --> 00:22:39,240
which is at the lung base in this upright patient.

273
00:22:39,800 --> 00:22:42,940
Also notice the interface between the fluid and the gas.

274
00:22:42,940 --> 00:22:49,100
It's a straight line, which reflects a gas fluid level.

275
00:22:50,980 --> 00:22:56,280
You can identify other abnormalities of the pleura besides pleural effusions and pneumothoraces.

276
00:22:57,180 --> 00:23:00,520
Specifically, you may be able to identify pleural thickening

277
00:23:00,520 --> 00:23:05,040
or the presence of calcified pleural plaques on radiography.

278
00:23:05,780 --> 00:23:10,220
Both of these images are frontal views of the chest that demonstrate pleural plaques.

279
00:23:10,220 --> 00:23:15,960
Along the diaphragmatic pleura on the left-sided image.

280
00:23:17,580 --> 00:23:22,500
And in the pleura of the midlung on the left-sided image.

281
00:23:23,300 --> 00:23:28,180
So the way that these appear will depend on what part of the pleura is calcified.

282
00:23:28,300 --> 00:23:33,160
So if you are looking at a portion of the pleura that's tangent to where you are,

283
00:23:33,160 --> 00:23:36,240
it will appear as a line or a band.

284
00:23:36,800 --> 00:23:39,720
Whereas if you're looking at the pleural plaque on FOS,

285
00:23:39,960 --> 00:23:45,520
it'll have a more bizarre type of appearance as it does on the image on the right.

286
00:23:46,100 --> 00:23:48,340
So in addition to effusions and pneumothoraces,

287
00:23:48,560 --> 00:23:51,900
we want to look for pleural thickening as well as pleural plaques.

288
00:23:58,030 --> 00:24:00,730
This question tests your knowledge of the relative sensitivities

289
00:24:00,730 --> 00:24:04,810
of the different radiographic views and detection of pleural effusion.

290
00:24:05,430 --> 00:24:07,450
Decubitus views are the most sensitive.

291
00:24:08,510 --> 00:24:12,990
In this example, there is a left-side-down lateral decubitus view of the chest

292
00:24:12,990 --> 00:24:20,250
showing a small pleural effusion layering along the dependent thoracic cavity in the pleural space.

293
00:24:20,250 --> 00:24:26,930
The upright lateral chest radiograph is the next most sensitive view.

294
00:24:27,610 --> 00:24:31,550
Here we see fluid collecting in the posterior costophrinic sulcus

295
00:24:31,550 --> 00:24:37,010
resulting in increased opacity and blunting of the costophrinic angle posteriorly.

296
00:24:37,890 --> 00:24:42,250
If enough fluid is present within the pleural space,

297
00:24:43,710 --> 00:24:50,010
blunting of the lateral costophrinic angles can be identified on the upright frontal view

298
00:24:50,010 --> 00:24:51,130
as shown here.

299
00:24:51,770 --> 00:24:55,710
There's a left pleural effusion with blunting of the left lateral costophrinic sulcus.

300
00:24:56,770 --> 00:25:03,130
And then finally, the most insensitive view for detection of pleural effusions

301
00:25:03,130 --> 00:25:07,570
is the supine frontal radiograph

302
00:25:07,570 --> 00:25:14,250
where we have the layering effect of pleural fluid resulting in this gradation of opacity

303
00:25:14,250 --> 00:25:20,330
from more opaque to more lucent as we go from lung base to lung apex.

304
00:25:22,130 --> 00:25:25,710
After evaluating the diaphragm and the pleural space,

305
00:25:25,950 --> 00:25:28,450
you want to move on to assessment of the lungs,

306
00:25:28,450 --> 00:25:31,950
which we'll talk about in the next module.

307
00:25:31,950 --> 00:25:34,430
After evaluation of the lungs,

308
00:25:35,130 --> 00:25:39,970
you want to round out or complete your thorough or systematic approach

309
00:25:39,970 --> 00:25:44,710
with assessment of the chest wall as well as the upper abdomen.

310
00:25:45,230 --> 00:25:49,790
Now really, you can include the upper abdomen in assessment of the diaphragms like we talked about

311
00:25:49,790 --> 00:25:53,210
because really the main thing you want to be looking for

312
00:25:53,210 --> 00:25:56,110
is the presence of free intraperitoneal gas,

313
00:25:56,210 --> 00:26:02,510
which will manifest itself as a lucency deep to the diaphragm.

314
00:26:02,670 --> 00:26:04,450
And we talk about this in other modules.

315
00:26:05,170 --> 00:26:07,290
But regarding assessment of the chest wall,

316
00:26:07,370 --> 00:26:10,910
we need to evaluate both the bones and the soft tissues.

317
00:26:11,650 --> 00:26:13,890
Number one, it's important to be thorough

318
00:26:13,890 --> 00:26:16,830
because these findings in the chest wall

319
00:26:16,830 --> 00:26:20,070
can be intrinsically important in and of themselves,

320
00:26:20,070 --> 00:26:25,010
but they can also be clues to underlying intrathoracic pathology.

321
00:26:25,190 --> 00:26:29,790
In other words, you might see some gas in the subcutaneous soft tissues,

322
00:26:30,130 --> 00:26:33,230
which may be indicative of an underlying pneumothorax.

323
00:26:35,500 --> 00:26:39,780
Now the point here is not to give a comprehensive discussion

324
00:26:39,780 --> 00:26:44,720
of all of the bone or soft tissue finding that you might encounter on a chest radiograph.

325
00:26:45,260 --> 00:26:48,120
Instead, it's merely to make you aware

326
00:26:48,120 --> 00:26:52,580
that you really need to assess the bones and soft tissues of the chest wall

327
00:26:52,580 --> 00:26:58,140
on every chest radiograph that you interpret as part of a systematic approach.

328
00:26:58,740 --> 00:27:01,700
In this frontal chest radiograph,

329
00:27:01,840 --> 00:27:03,400
there's a lot going on.

330
00:27:03,500 --> 00:27:06,180
There's underlying lung pathology, some lines and tubes.

331
00:27:06,960 --> 00:27:08,100
And if we're not careful, though,

332
00:27:08,120 --> 00:27:12,580
we can miss the overall big picture of the abnormal bone.

333
00:27:12,580 --> 00:27:15,380
So there's a diffuse mottled appearance of the bones.

334
00:27:16,120 --> 00:27:19,640
And more focally, there's destruction of a posterior right rib

335
00:27:19,640 --> 00:27:21,980
that we can identify here.

336
00:27:22,560 --> 00:27:25,160
And in an up-close view, I think you can see the detail.

337
00:27:25,320 --> 00:27:26,980
You can see rib destruction.

338
00:27:27,160 --> 00:27:31,160
So this is a patient with osseous metastasis.

339
00:27:34,150 --> 00:27:38,970
In this example, we identify gas within the soft tissues of the right chest wall.

340
00:27:38,970 --> 00:27:42,650
Now, this finding in and of itself may not be important,

341
00:27:43,030 --> 00:27:48,190
but it can be a clue to underlying disease that might be significant.

342
00:27:48,650 --> 00:27:51,050
So in this example, on an oblique view,

343
00:27:51,170 --> 00:27:56,530
we can again see the gas within the right chest wall of soft tissues.

344
00:27:57,510 --> 00:27:59,470
But we also notice that there's a rib fracture,

345
00:28:00,030 --> 00:28:02,590
which again, in and of itself, might not be that important.

346
00:28:03,110 --> 00:28:06,930
However, this patient also had a very tiny right pneumothorax

347
00:28:06,930 --> 00:28:11,870
that could be important that may have not otherwise been identified

348
00:28:11,870 --> 00:28:15,670
unless you were really scrutinizing or looking for this pneumothorax,

349
00:28:16,010 --> 00:28:20,330
which you would be if you identified these ancillary findings

350
00:28:20,330 --> 00:28:23,290
of soft tissue gas and underlying rib fractures.

351
00:28:23,650 --> 00:28:29,290
In this module, we discussed evaluation of the diaphragm,

352
00:28:29,550 --> 00:28:30,970
pleural space, and chest wall.

353
00:28:32,170 --> 00:28:36,650
Regarding the diaphragm, we reviewed the radiographic findings

354
00:28:36,650 --> 00:28:41,390
of potential causes of an elevated diaphragm,

355
00:28:41,390 --> 00:28:46,130
or at least the appearance of an elevated diaphragm on chest radiography,

356
00:28:46,530 --> 00:28:51,710
including true diaphragmatic elevation caused by paralysis and eventration,

357
00:28:52,130 --> 00:28:55,790
as well as the apparent elevation of the diaphragm

358
00:28:55,790 --> 00:28:57,570
caused by a subpalmonic effusion.

359
00:28:57,570 --> 00:29:05,150
We talked about how the radiographic appearance of pleural effusions and pneumothoraces vary

360
00:29:05,150 --> 00:29:08,350
depending on the position of the patient,

361
00:29:08,590 --> 00:29:12,990
with pleural fluid settling to the dependent portion of the pleural space,

362
00:29:13,530 --> 00:29:20,170
and pleural gas moving to the non-dependent portion of the pleural space.

363
00:29:20,310 --> 00:29:24,910
And again, these will vary depending on the position of the patient.

364
00:29:24,910 --> 00:29:28,590
So with pleural effusions, we expect increased opacity,

365
00:29:29,250 --> 00:29:31,550
blunting of the costophrenic angles,

366
00:29:31,850 --> 00:29:37,070
and the meniscus effect caused by the fluid layering against the chest wall.

367
00:29:37,490 --> 00:29:41,770
We discussed the relative sensitivities for different radiographic views

368
00:29:41,770 --> 00:29:44,170
regarding the detection of pleural effusions,

369
00:29:44,270 --> 00:29:46,770
with the cubitus views being the most sensitive,

370
00:29:47,690 --> 00:29:50,650
and lateral upright views being more sensitive

371
00:29:50,650 --> 00:29:53,290
than frontal upright views of the chest.

372
00:29:53,290 --> 00:29:55,950
Regarding pneumothoraces,

373
00:29:56,190 --> 00:29:59,010
we talked about detection of the visceral pleural line

374
00:29:59,010 --> 00:30:02,750
as being really the most specific finding for a pneumothorax.

375
00:30:03,210 --> 00:30:05,570
We talked about the deep sulcus sign

376
00:30:05,570 --> 00:30:11,450
as a radiographic sign of a pneumothorax in a supine patient.

377
00:30:11,630 --> 00:30:13,710
And again, remember that in a supine patient,

378
00:30:13,950 --> 00:30:17,010
the most non-dependent portion of the thoracic cavity

379
00:30:17,010 --> 00:30:18,670
is at the lung base.

380
00:30:18,670 --> 00:30:22,070
And then finally, we rounded out our discussion

381
00:30:22,070 --> 00:30:26,850
with a brief evaluation of the chest wall

382
00:30:26,850 --> 00:30:30,070
with attention not only to the bones of the chest wall,

383
00:30:30,170 --> 00:30:31,670
but also to the soft tissues,

384
00:30:31,930 --> 00:30:35,270
given that these findings may be intrinsically important,

385
00:30:35,730 --> 00:30:40,790
or they may be an indicator of some underlying intrathoracic pathology

386
00:30:40,790 --> 00:30:43,090
that might not otherwise be evident.

387
00:30:43,090 --> 00:30:46,230
That brings us to the end of this unit.

388
00:30:46,670 --> 00:30:48,870
Please complete the corresponding quiz questions

389
00:30:48,870 --> 00:30:50,650
to receive credit for this course.

